{% sec 1, "Four Ways to Make Money", "Where does trading profit actually come from?", "Four ways to make money" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Picture a corner shop. It makes money in four quite different ways. It charges a little more than the supermarket for being open at 11pm when you've run out of milk: you pay for convenience. It buys crisps from the wholesaler for less than the supermarket's shelf price and sells them in between: a price gap between two places. It notices that umbrellas sell when rain is forecast and stocks more on cloudy days: a small pattern that pays off over many sales. And it sells extended warranties on kettles, collecting a small fee every time and occasionally paying out for a broken one: being paid to carry someone else's risk.</p>
    <p>Trading firms have the same four engines. Deltashark's point is that reading any strategy starts with asking which engine it runs on, because each has its own risk shape and its own limit on size:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Paid for</th><th>Examples</th><th>Risk shape</th><th>What limits its size</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>{% gl "liquidity provision" %}Liquidity provision{% endgl %}</td><td>Being there to trade immediately: the spread</td><td>Market making in shares, options, FX</td><td>Steady, with sharp losses when informed traders pick you off or inventory moves against you</td><td>How much flow there is to serve</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "arbitrage" %}Arbitrage{% endgl %} and relative value</td><td>Enforcing the {% gl "law of one price" %}law of one price{% endgl %}</td><td>An index against its shares, futures against spot, related bonds against each other</td><td>Small, nearly certain margins, with the tail risk that "identical" things drift apart in a crisis</td><td>How many mispricings there are, and the leverage needed to make them worth it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Statistical edges</td><td>Being slightly right, many times</td><td>Momentum, short-term reversal, order-flow signals</td><td>Noisy, roughly symmetric; the 51% coin from What Quant Trading Is</td><td>Market impact as trades get bigger</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "risk premium" %}Risk premia{% endgl %}</td><td>Holding risks others want to get rid of</td><td>Carry, credit spreads, selling options</td><td>Steady gains with occasional large losses: {% gl "negative skew" %}negative skew{% endgl %}</td><td>How much of the risk the firm can survive</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
